    "author_association": "COLLABORATOR",
    "body": "> Thanks for the quick response!\n> \n> I\u2019d like to leverage the W4A4 (block-scaled FP4 E2M1) speed-ups on Blackwell Tensor Cores and have Triton handle all of the usual FP4 array ops, i.e. GEMM dispatch, transposes, point-wise adds, tensor printing, etc. With the NVIDIA NVFP4 scheme (and compatible with the OCP MXFP4 spec). Could you clarify whether \u201cfp4x2\u201d in Triton already means two packed E2M1 values per byte, does it allow W4A4 tensor core utilization, and what\u2019s on the roadmap for first-class FP4 support (including printing and reshape at element granularity)?\n\nto be clear, `\u201cfp4x2\u201d` doesn't exist in triton at the moment. You can currently use `tl.dot_scaled` op to access blackwell mxfp tensor cores, this currently takes a i8 tensor which is two packed e2m1. Transpose, point-wise adds and others are not natively supported in HW therefore they won't be added in Triton. Again the only thing fp4x2 type gives you is syntactic sugar and maybe better printing, it wouldn't add any functionality.\n",
    "created_at": "2025-06-18T18:24:33Z",
